1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,513
Lantern Row: a night market on NEAR.
Run a stall and out-trade the merchants.

2
00:00:06,713 --> 00:00:14,683
One tap opens a stall. No wallet, no sign-up: your
browser makes a key, and that key owns the stall.

3
00:00:14,883 --> 00:00:18,717
Minted on chain. There it is, on the street.

4
00:00:19,199 --> 00:00:27,333
*Restock* fills the counter from the Dock.
*Smart prices* sets what sells tonight.

5
00:00:27,383 --> 00:00:32,119
Each card says how many want the good,
and how many will buy at your price.

6
00:00:32,319 --> 00:00:40,155
Every 20 seconds, twelve villagers walk the row with
their own wallets and tastes. Coins fly when they buy.

7
00:00:40,632 --> 00:00:44,878
The night report: what sold, who walked away, and why.

8
00:00:44,928 --> 00:00:51,020
Then one call to make. A festival of
lamp oil starts next night: stock up?

9
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:56,826
The festival comes, with bunting and
fireworks, and everyone wants lamp oil.

10
00:00:58,812 --> 00:01:05,430
The next report says how the call
went. When it paid off: *stamped*.

11
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:12,511
The deed. A stall is an NFT that holds its own
coin and goods: sell it, and all of it goes along.

12
00:01:12,561 --> 00:01:18,558
The founder earns 5% of every resale.
List it, and the sign goes up.

13
00:01:18,758 --> 00:01:23,619
*For sale* on the street, for anyone with the coin.

14
00:01:23,819 --> 00:01:29,132
Ranks: your stall against the six merchants,
and every other player on the row.

15
00:01:30,341 --> 00:01:36,361
Every tap was a real transaction on NEAR
testnet. The proof drawer lists each one.

16
00:01:38,376 --> 00:01:46,870
Why NEAR. The contract has no keys: nobody can patch
a rule or mint coin. One closed economy for everyone.

17
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:51,607
Stalls are NFTs that own money and
stock. The token is the account.

18
00:01:51,657 --> 00:01:57,130
And no wallet: the browser's key
signs, scoped relay keys pay the gas.

19
00:01:57,180 --> 00:02:01,594
Where would you put your stall: by
the gate, or where the rent is low?

